          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I feel sisterly love for you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Jane directly avows affection for St. John, explicitly qualifying it as sisterly rather than romantic or marital love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The love expression itself makes no undertaking. Jane's separate offer to accompany St. John as an assistant must not be transferred onto it; she expressly denies having a binding obligation, and St. John rejects her proposed non-marital arrangement.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With me,\\nthen, it seems, you cannot go\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within a reasoned explanation of Jane's position, not as a reflex-like or uncontrolled discharge of affect.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"Its core force is adequately captured as an avowal of specifically sisterly affection. Although it also helps explain and soften Jane's refusal, no distinct residual mode is central to the expression itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane addresses St. John during a dispute over his proposal that she marry him and accompany him to India. She distinguishes affectionate, sister-like attachment from romantic or marital love and uses the avowal to explain why she had been willing to venture abroad with him, while refusing marriage and denying any obligation to go with strangers.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression undertakes nothing. Separately, Jane had offered to accompany St. John as his assistant or sister, not as his wife. St. John rejects that arrangement as unsuitable and suggests service with another missionary; Jane then denies that she made a formal promise or remains obliged to go. Thus the separate proposed undertaking has disputed terms and no mutual uptake in the form Jane offered.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre. The classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence clearly establishes the avowed affection as sisterly and non-romantic in this scene. It does not establish every broader nuance of Jane and St. John's relationship beyond the supplied passages, and the separate question of travel to India remains conditional and contested.\"}"
        }
      ],
